//
// loft#1221's first REFUSAL cell. The positive half is
// `1221-an-admissible-source-reaches-a-route.loft`; the second refusal is
// `1221c-a-whole-keyed-collection-has-no-append-route.loft`.
//
// Three files rather than two, and the split is the PASS the two checks run in rather than
// tidiness: this cell's refusal is @PLAN52's ambiguity check, which is not pass-gated and so
// fires in pass 1, and a pass-1 error stops the file before pass 2 runs. Put beside it, the
// keyed-whole cell — whose check is `!first_pass` — never reports at all, and its
// `@EXPECT_ERROR` goes unmatched for a reason that has nothing to do with the fix.
//
// Both cells are sources the `+=` routes could not place, and both used to be answered with
// silence. What separates them from loft#1215 is that neither is a type error — the value is
// exactly what the collection holds, or is the collection itself — so the refusal is about
// which OPERATION exists, and the two messages differ accordingly.
//
// @falsified-at: e695a6f5 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0
//   The direction inverts for an `@EXPECT_ERROR` file: the corpus runner scores it by whether
//   its expectation MATCHES, so the parent — where nothing fires — is the run that exits 1. The
//   cell COMPILES and runs there, so a naive exit reading of the PROGRAM would call it clean:
//   it grows its vector by THREE where one record was written, and says nothing.
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see TESTING.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.
//   HOLDS: on --interpret, assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal are equal on both trees,
//   measured by `scripts/falsify.sh --bulk` against e695a6f5; falsify prints only the
//   channels that MOVE, and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on both. The
//   exit/expectations channel carries this one on that backend. The native side was not
//   scored in that run.

enum Tag1221b { Anon1221b, Named1221b { nm: text } }

struct B1221b { items: vector<Tag1221b> }
// A VARIANT is an element of a vector over its enum — `(C-Var)`, and the argument, return and
// struct-field positions all accept it. So this is a bare-element append and owes the reader
// @PLAN52's brackets, the same message the plain element spelling has always earned.
//
// It got nothing. The ambiguity check asked `is_equal`, which reads `Reference(Named1221b)` and
// `Enum(Tag1221b, …)` as unrelated, so the statement fell past every route and the generic path
// grew the vector by THREE. That the count was wrong rather than merely surprising is why this
// cell is a refusal and not a documentation fix.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: vector `+= elem` is ambiguous
fn test_1221b_a_bare_variant_append_is_ambiguous() {
  b1221b = B1221b { items: [] };
  b1221b.items += Named1221b { nm: "one" };
  assert(len(b1221b.items) == 1, "unreached");
}
